{"name":"Berry Crush","hex":"#aa6772","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"},{"term":"crush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent collision or compression; a crash; destruction; ruin.","Violent pressure, as of a moving crowd."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To press between two hard objects; to squeeze so as to alter the natural shape or integrity, or to force together into a mass.","To reduce to fine particles by pounding or grinding."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crush (drink)","blocks":[{"text":"Crush is a brand of carbonated soft drinks originally created as an orange soda, Orange-Crush. The brand is owned and marketed internationally by Keurig Dr Pepper, but is marketed by PepsiCo in North America. It was created in 1911, 29 years before The Coca-Cola Company's Fanta, by beverage and extract chemist Neil C. Ward. Most flavors of Crush are caffeine-free."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Crush_Soda_Logo_New.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crush_(drink)","wikidata":"Q9943"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}